router: match stored RFC 9096 PIOs against shorter address prefixes

A PIO is stored with the advertised prefix length (at least 64), but the
interface address it came from may be shorter (lan ip6assign 60 gives br-lan
a ULA /60). The equal-length check never matched, so every RA re-added the
live prefix as stale and advertised it with zero lifetimes. Compare over the
longer of the two lengths, at least 64, as upstream router_compare_pio_addr()
does.

--- a/src/router.c	2025-10-02 12:52:15.000000000 +0000
+++ b/src/router.c	2026-09-24 23:37:13.895221711 +0000
@@ -653,8 +653,18 @@
 			for (size_t j = 0; j < valid_addr_cnt; j++) {
 				struct odhcpd_ipaddr *cur_addr = &addrs[j];
 
-				if (cur_pio->length == cur_addr->prefix &&
-					!odhcpd_bmemcmp(&cur_pio->prefix, &cur_addr->addr.in6, cur_pio->length)) {
+				/*
+				 * The PIO is stored with the advertised length, which is
+				 * at least 64 even for a shorter address prefix (e.g. a
+				 * ULA /60 from ip6assign 60): compare the longer length.
+				 */
+				uint8_t cmp_len = cur_pio->length > cur_addr->prefix ?
+						cur_pio->length : cur_addr->prefix;
+
+				if (cmp_len < 64)
+					cmp_len = 64;
+
+				if (!odhcpd_bmemcmp(&cur_pio->prefix, &cur_addr->addr.in6, cmp_len)) {
 					pio_found = true;
 					break;
 				}
